          `Data Link layer OSI model ki **Layer 2** hai. Iska kaam hai Network layer se aaye packet ko ek **frame** mein badalna aur use ek hi network ke andar sahi device tak pahunchaana. Aap isko ek local courier ki tarah samajh sakte ho: Network layer parcel ko sheher tak le aata hai, Data Link layer use sheher ke andar sahi ghar tak pahuncha deta hai. Is layer ka kaam sirf ek network ke andar hai — doosre network mein jaana Network layer ka kaam hai.

Data Link layer ke teen main kaam hain. Pehla, **framing**: packet ke aage aur peeche flag aur header-trailer jodna, taaki receiver ko pata chale ki frame kahan se shuru aur kahan khatam hota hai. Doosra, **physical addressing**: har device ka ek **MAC address** hota hai, aur frame par source aur destination MAC address likha hota hai. Teesra, **error detection**: frame ke end mein ek check number (CRC) hota hai, jisse receiver ko pata chalta hai ki frame raaste mein kharab toh nahi hua.

Is layer par do sub-layer hoti hain: **LLC (Logical Link Control)** upar wali sub-layer hai jo Network layer se baat karti hai, aur **MAC (Media Access Control)** neeche wali sub-layer hai jo medium se baat karti hai aur MAC address ko handle karti hai. Exam mein aksar poochha jaata hai "Data Link layer ki sub-layers kaunsi hain?" — jawab hai LLC aur MAC.

Data Link layer ka sabse mashhoor protocol hai **Ethernet** — jo aapke office aur ghar ke wired network mein chalta hai. Wireless mein **Wi-Fi (802.11)** chalta hai, jo Data Link layer ka hi ek standard hai. Dono ka kaam ek hai: frame banana aur sahi device tak pahunchaana, bas medium alag hai.`,
          `The Data Link layer is **Layer 2** of the OSI model. Its job is to turn a packet from the Network layer into a **frame** and deliver it to the right device inside one network. You can picture it as a local courier: the Network layer brings the parcel to the city, and the Data Link layer delivers it to the right house inside the city. This layer works only inside one network — going to another network is the Network layer's job.

The Data Link layer has three main jobs. First, **framing**: adding a flag and a header-trailer before and after the packet, so the receiver knows where the frame starts and where it ends. Second, **physical addressing**: every device has a **MAC address**, and the frame carries the source and destination MAC addresses. Third, **error detection**: the end of the frame carries a check number (CRC), which tells the receiver whether the frame was damaged on the way.

This layer has two sub-layers: **LLC (Logical Link Control)** is the upper sub-layer that talks to the Network layer, and **MAC (Media Access Control)** is the lower sub-layer that talks to the medium and handles the MAC address. Exams often ask "What are the sub-layers of the Data Link layer?" — the answer is LLC and MAC.

The most famous protocol of this layer is **Ethernet** — it runs in the wired networks of your office and home. Wireless uses **Wi-Fi (802.11)**, which is also a Data Link layer standard. Both do the same job: build a frame and deliver it to the right device; only the medium differs.`
