          `Ab hum ek poora ARP flow dry run karenge: aapka computer (192.168.1.1) kisi doosre computer (192.168.1.5) ko data bhejna chahta hai, par use uska MAC nahi pata. Neeche wala code is process ko print karta hai — pehle code padho, phir dry run se milao.

**Step 1 — Table check karo:** Computer ki ARP table mein do entries hain: 192.168.1.1 ka MAC \`AA:BB:CC:00:00:01\` aur 192.168.1.2 ka MAC \`AA:BB:CC:00:00:02\`. Target hai 192.168.1.5. Table mein 192.168.1.5 nahi hai — **cache miss** hua.

**Step 2 — ARP request (broadcast):** Computer ek broadcast message bhejta hai jo network ke har device ko jaata hai: "Who has 192.168.1.5? Tell 192.168.1.1" — matlab "192.168.1.5 kiska hai? 192.168.1.1 ko batao." Yeh message har device ko milta hai, par sirf 192.168.1.5 wala device jawab dega. Baaki sab message ko ignore kar denge.

**Step 3 — ARP reply (unicast):** 192.168.1.5 wala computer jawab bhejta hai — sirf poochhne wale ko, sab ko nahi. Jawab mein uska MAC hota hai: \`AA:BB:CC:00:00:05\`. Yeh reply unicast hota hai, broadcast nahi, kyunki ab poochhne wale ka MAC pata chal gaya hai (request mein likha tha).

**Step 4 — Table update:** Computer nayi entry ko apni ARP table mein likh leta hai. Ab table mein 3 entries hain. Agli baar jab 192.168.1.5 ko data bhejna ho, toh table mein MAC mil jaayega aur koi broadcast nahi hoga — kaam turant ho jaayega. Yahi ARP cache ka fayda hai: ek baar poochho, phir baar-baar use karo.`,
          `Now let us dry run a complete ARP flow: your computer (192.168.1.1) wants to send data to another computer (192.168.1.5), but it does not know its MAC. The code below prints this process — read the code first, then match it with the dry run.

**Step 1 — Check the table:** The computer's ARP table has two entries: the MAC of 192.168.1.1 is \`AA:BB:CC:00:00:01\` and the MAC of 192.168.1.2 is \`AA:BB:CC:00:00:02\`. The target is 192.168.1.5. The table has no 192.168.1.5 — this is a **cache miss**.

**Step 2 — ARP request (broadcast):** The computer sends a broadcast message that goes to every device on the network: "Who has 192.168.1.5? Tell 192.168.1.1" — meaning "Whose IP is 192.168.1.5? Tell 192.168.1.1." Every device receives this message, but only the device with 192.168.1.5 will answer. The rest ignore the message.

**Step 3 — ARP reply (unicast):** The computer with 192.168.1.5 sends a reply — only to the asker, not to everyone. The reply carries its MAC: \`AA:BB:CC:00:00:05\`. This reply is unicast, not broadcast, because the asker's MAC is now known (it was written in the request).

**Step 4 — Table update:** The computer writes the new entry into its ARP table. Now the table has 3 entries. The next time data must go to 192.168.1.5, the MAC will be found in the table and there will be no broadcast — the job finishes at once. That is the benefit of the ARP cache: ask once, then reuse it again and again.`
