          `IPv4 mein sabse zyada galtiyan chaar cheezon par hoti hain.

**Galti 1: Network address ko host samajhna.** 192.168.1.0 network address hai — yeh kisi device ko nahi diya ja sakta. Log aksar 192.168.1.0 ko pehla computer samajh lete hain — galat. Pehla host 192.168.1.1 hai. Aise hi 192.168.1.255 broadcast address hai — yeh bhi kisi device ko nahi diya ja sakta. Exam mein aksar poochha jaata hai "192.168.1.0/24 mein kitne usable hosts hain?" — jawab 254 hai, 256 nahi.

**Galti 2: TTL ko time samajhna.** TTL ka naam "Time To Live" hai, par yeh time nahi, **hops** gin ta hai. Har router se guzarte time TTL 1 kam hota hai. Agar TTL 64 hai, toh packet zyada se zyada 64 router se guzar sakta hai. Log aksar bolte hain "TTL 64 second hai" — galat. TTL 64 hops hai. Yeh naam purana hai — pehle TTL seconds mein hota tha, ab hops mein hota hai.

**Galti 3: Header checksum ko poora packet check samajhna.** IPv4 header mein **Header Checksum** hota hai, jo sirf header ko check karta hai — payload ko nahi. Payload ki jaanch TCP ya UDP ka checksum karta hai. Log aksar bolte hain "IP checksum poore packet ko check karta hai" — galat. Sirf header check hota hai, isliye IP checksum tez hota hai.

**Galti 4: Classful addressing ko aaj ka standard samajhna.** Class A, B, C ka system purana hai — aaj **CIDR (Classless)** use hota hai, jismein /24, /16 jaise prefix likhe jaate hain. Par exam mein classes abhi bhi poochhi jaati hain, isliye yaad rakho: Class A 1-126 (pehla octet), Class B 128-191, Class C 192-223, Class D 224-239 (multicast), Class E 240-255 (research). 127 loopback ke liye hai — yeh kisi class mein nahi aata.`,
          `The most common IPv4 mistakes happen on four points.

**Mistake 1: Treating the network address as a host.** 192.168.1.0 is the network address — it cannot be given to any device. People often think 192.168.1.0 is the first computer — wrong. The first host is 192.168.1.1. In the same way, 192.168.1.255 is the broadcast address — it also cannot be given to any device. Exams often ask "How many usable hosts are in 192.168.1.0/24?" — the answer is 254, not 256.

**Mistake 2: Thinking TTL is time.** TTL is named "Time To Live", but it counts **hops**, not time. It drops by 1 at every router it passes. If TTL is 64, the packet can pass at most 64 routers. People often say "TTL is 64 seconds" — wrong. TTL is 64 hops. The name is old — TTL used to be in seconds, but now it is in hops.

**Mistake 3: Thinking the header checksum checks the whole packet.** The IPv4 header holds a **Header Checksum**, which checks only the header — not the payload. The payload is checked by the TCP or UDP checksum. People often say "the IP checksum checks the whole packet" — wrong. Only the header is checked, which is why the IP checksum is fast.

**Mistake 4: Treating classful addressing as today's standard.** The Class A, B, C system is old — today **CIDR (Classless)** is used, with prefixes like /24 and /16. But exams still ask about classes, so remember: Class A 1-126 (first octet), Class B 128-191, Class C 192-223, Class D 224-239 (multicast), Class E 240-255 (research). 127 is for loopback — it belongs to no class.`
