          `**IPv6 (Internet Protocol version 6)** IPv4 ka naya version hai, jo isliye bana kyunki IPv4 ke address khatam ho gaye. IPv4 mein address **32 bits** ka hota hai aur total sirf 4.3 billion address hain. IPv6 mein address **128 bits** ka hota hai — matlab total addresses hain 2 ki power 128, lagbhag **340 undecillion**. Yeh number itna bada hai ki duniya ke har device ko, har ret ke dane ko bhi, apna alag address mil sakta hai.

IPv6 address **hexadecimal** mein likha jaata hai — 8 groups, har group 4 hex digits ka, aur groups ko colon (:) se joda jaata hai. Jaise: \`2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329\`. Har group 16 bits ka hota hai, aur 8 x 16 = 128 bits. IPv4 ke dot (.) ki jagah yahan colon (:) use hota hai — yahi sabse pehli pehchan hai.

IPv6 address ko chhota likhne ke **do rules** hain. Pehla: har group ke aage ke zero hata do — \`0db8\` ko \`db8\` likh sakte ho, \`0042\` ko \`42\` likh sakte ho. Doosra: lagatar zero wale groups ko ek baar \`::\` se badal do — \`2001:db8:0:0:0:ff00:42:8329\` ko \`2001:db8::ff00:42:8329\` likh sakte ho. Par \`::\` ek address mein **sirf ek baar** use ho sakta hai, warna samajh nahi aayega ki kitne zero chhupaye gaye hain. Neeche wala code full form aur short form dono dikhata hai.

IPv6 header IPv4 se **simple** hai: sirf **40 bytes** ka fixed header, koi options nahi, koi header checksum nahi, aur koi fragmentation nahi (fragmentation sirf sender karta hai). IPv4 header mein 13 fields the, IPv6 mein sirf 8 fields hain. Header simple hone se router ko packet process karne mein kam time lagta hai — isliye IPv6 tez hota hai.`,
          `**IPv6 (Internet Protocol version 6)** is the new version of IPv4, built because IPv4 addresses ran out. In IPv4, an address is **32 bits** and there are only 4.3 billion addresses in total. In IPv6, an address is **128 bits** — the total addresses are 2 to the power 128, about **340 undecillion**. That number is so big that every device in the world, even every grain of sand, could have its own address.

An IPv6 address is written in **hexadecimal** — 8 groups, each group 4 hex digits, joined by a colon (:). For example: \`2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329\`. Each group is 16 bits, and 8 x 16 = 128 bits. A colon (:) is used here instead of the IPv4 dot (.) — that is the first way to recognize it.

There are **two rules** for writing an IPv6 address short. First: drop the leading zeros in each group — \`0db8\` can be written as \`db8\`, and \`0042\` as \`42\`. Second: replace consecutive zero groups once with \`::\` — \`2001:db8:0:0:0:ff00:42:8329\` can be written as \`2001:db8::ff00:42:8329\`. But \`::\` can be used only **once** in an address, or nobody can tell how many zeros were hidden. The code below shows both the full form and the short form.

The IPv6 header is **simpler** than IPv4: a fixed **40-byte** header, no options, no header checksum, and no fragmentation (only the sender fragments). The IPv4 header had 13 fields; IPv6 has only 8 fields. A simple header means a router spends less time processing a packet — so IPv6 is faster.`
