          `**DHCP** ka full form hai **Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol**. Yeh ek aisa protocol hai jo network mein naye device ko automatically IP address aur baaki settings deta hai. Jab aapka phone ya laptop Wi-Fi se judta hai, toh use ek IP address chahiye hota hai taaki woh internet par data bhej aur paa sake. Bina IP address ke aapka device network par bol hi nahi sakta. DHCP server yahi kaam karta hai — woh ek pool mein rakhe IP addresses mein se ek khaali address chun kar device ko de deta hai.

DHCP sirf IP address hi nahi deta. Uske saath woh **subnet mask**, **default gateway** (aapke router ka address, jaise \`192.168.1.1\`) aur **DNS server** ka address bhi bhejta hai. Matlab ek hi jhatke mein device ko network par kaam karne ke liye jo kuch chahiye, sab mil jaata hai. Aapko kuch bhi haath se type nahi karna padta. Isi liye ghar ke Wi-Fi par naya phone judte hi internet chalne lagta hai.

IP address hamesha ke liye nahi milta — woh **lease** par milta hai, matlab ek fixed time ke liye, jaise 3600 seconds (1 ghanta) ya 86400 seconds (1 din). Lease khatam hone se pehle device dobara maangta hai ki lease badha do. Agar device network chhod de, toh uska IP address pool mein wapas chala jaata hai aur kisi aur device ko mil sakta hai. Isi liye DHCP ko **dynamic** kehte hain — address aate-jaate rehte hain, kisi ek device se chipke nahi rehte.`,
          `**DHCP** stands for **Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol**. It is a protocol that automatically gives a new device on a network an IP address and the other settings it needs. When your phone or laptop joins Wi-Fi, it needs an IP address so it can send and receive data on the internet. Without an IP address, your device cannot speak on the network at all. The DHCP server does this job — it picks a free address from a pool of addresses and hands it to the device.

DHCP gives more than just an IP address. Along with it, the server also sends the **subnet mask**, the **default gateway** (your router's address, such as \`192.168.1.1\`), and the address of the **DNS server**. So in one go, the device gets everything it needs to work on the network. You do not have to type anything by hand. That is why a new phone starts using the internet the moment it joins your home Wi-Fi.

The IP address is not given forever — it is given on a **lease**, which means for a fixed time, such as 3600 seconds (1 hour) or 86400 seconds (1 day). Before the lease ends, the device asks again to extend it. If the device leaves the network, its IP address goes back into the pool and can be given to another device. That is why DHCP is called **dynamic** — addresses keep coming and going and are not stuck to one device.`
