          `The simplest example is your **home Wi-Fi**. Your home router acts as the DHCP server. When a friend visits and enters the Wi-Fi password, their phone at once gets an address like \`192.168.1.105\`. You do not have to change anything in the router settings. A router's pool usually holds addresses from \`192.168.1.100\` to \`192.168.1.200\`, which means up to 100 devices can join.

A second example is a **college hostel or an office**. There, 500 people come and go every day. If every device's IP had to be set by hand, the IT team would spend the whole day doing only that. With DHCP, every laptop takes its own address the moment it joins and gives it back when it leaves. The lease time is kept short here, such as 8 hours, so a new student arriving tomorrow can take an address from tomorrow's pool.

A third example is **mobile data**. When your phone joins 4G or 5G, the mobile company's DHCP-like system gives your phone an IP. Toggling flight mode on and off can give you a new IP each time, which is why websites cannot identify you by IP alone. The same thing happens in a hotel — your laptop gets an address at check-in, and at check-out that address goes back into the pool.`
