          `**Mistake 1: "The IP from DHCP is mine forever" — no.** A DHCP address comes on a lease. If your laptop stayed off for 2 days and the lease was 1 day, that same IP may already have been given to someone else by the time you return. That is why devices that must always keep one address, such as a server or a printer, get a **static IP** (set by hand) or a **DHCP reservation** (a fixed address tied to their MAC).

**Mistake 2: Remembering the DORA order backwards.** In interviews, people forget that the Offer comes before the Request. Remember it this way: first the device **asks** (Discover), then the server **offers** (Offer), then the device **chooses** (Request), then the server **confirms** (Acknowledge). Discover and Request both travel from client to server; Offer and Acknowledge both travel from server to client.

**Mistake 3: "DHCP only gives an IP" — no.** DHCP also gives the gateway and DNS. If a device got an IP but no gateway, it can only talk inside the local network, not on the internet. Often Wi-Fi shows "connected but no internet" for exactly this reason — the IP arrived, but the gateway or DNS was incomplete.

**Mistake 4: Two DHCP servers on one network.** If you plug in two routers at home with DHCP on in both, they start handing out addresses from different pools, and two devices can receive the same IP — an **IP conflict**. One network should have only one DHCP server; any extra router should run in access point mode.`
