          `**Slow start** ka matlab hai connection shuru mein dheere bhejo, phir speed badhao. Sender ek **congestion window (cwnd)** rakhta hai jo batata hai ki ek baar mein kitne segment bhej sakte hain. Shuru mein cwnd = 1 segment. Har ACK aane par cwnd badhta hai, aur har RTT mein cwnd **double** ho jaata hai: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. Yeh exponential badhotri hai, isliye naam ke ulta slow start asal mein bahut tez badhta hai. Jab cwnd **ssthresh** (slow start threshold) ko chhu leta hai, toh badhotri slow ho jaati hai — har RTT mein sirf +1 segment. Ise **congestion avoidance** kehte hain.

**AIMD** ka matlab hai **Additive Increase, Multiplicative Decrease**. Packet loss nahi hai toh har RTT mein cwnd mein sirf 1 jodo (additive increase). Packet loss hua toh cwnd ko **aadha** kar do (multiplicative decrease). Yeh rule saare sender ko fair rakhta hai — jo zyada le raha hai, woh zyada kam karta hai, aur dheere-dheere sab barabar ho jaate hain.

**What if slow start na hota?** Sender pehle hi second mein poora data bhej deta aur network jam ho jaata — packet khote, timeout hote, aur sab dobara bhejte. Slow start network ko "test" karta hai ki kitni speed chalegi. Neeche wala code slow start dikhata hai: cwnd 1 se shuru hota hai, 16 tak double hota hai, phir +1 se badhta hai.`,
          `**Slow start** means: send slowly at the start of a connection, then increase the speed. The sender keeps a **congestion window (cwnd)** that tells how many segments can be sent at once. At the start, cwnd = 1 segment. With every ACK, cwnd grows, and in every RTT cwnd **doubles**: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. This growth is exponential, so despite its name, slow start actually grows very fast. When cwnd reaches **ssthresh** (the slow start threshold), growth slows down — only +1 segment per RTT. This is called **congestion avoidance**.

**AIMD** stands for **Additive Increase, Multiplicative Decrease**. If there is no packet loss, add just 1 to cwnd every RTT (additive increase). If a packet is lost, **halve** cwnd (multiplicative decrease). This rule keeps all senders fair — whoever is taking more reduces more, and slowly everyone becomes equal.

**What if there were no slow start?** The sender would push all the data in the first second, the network would jam, packets would be lost, timeouts would follow, and everybody would resend. Slow start "tests" the network to learn how much speed it can take. The code below shows slow start: cwnd starts at 1, doubles up to 16, and then grows by +1.`
