          `Sabse seedha example **video call** hai. Video call mein har frame ek packet hai. Agar ek frame kho jaaye, toh use dobara bhejne ka koi matlab nahi — tab tak screen par agla frame aa chuka hota hai. Dobara bhejne se sirf delay badhega aur call atakne lagegi. Isliye video call UDP par chalta hai: khoya frame bhool jao, agla frame dikhao. Ek chhota sa glitch chalega, par rukawat nahi chalegi.

Doosra example hai **online game**. Game mein aapki position har 50 millisecond mein update hoti hai. Agar ek update kho jaaye, toh agla update waise bhi aa raha hai — purani position dobara bhejne ka koi fayda nahi. TCP hota toh khoya packet dobara aane tak agle packet ruk jaate (head-of-line blocking) aur game lag karne lagta. UDP mein aisa nahi hota.

Teesra example hai **DNS**. Aap jab website ka naam type karte ho, toh ek chhota sa DNS query jaata hai aur ek chhota sa jawab aata hai — ek hi packet mein kaam ho jaata hai. Iske liye TCP ka handshake (3 packet) bahut mehenga padta. Isi liye DNS aam taur par UDP par chalta hai. Agar jawab nahi aaya, toh app khud dobara query bhej deti hai — reliability app ke haath mein hai, protocol ke haath mein nahi.`,
          `The simplest example is a **video call**. In a video call, every frame is a packet. If one frame is lost, resending it makes no sense — by then the next frame is already on screen. Resending would only add delay and make the call freeze. So video calls run on UDP: forget the lost frame, show the next one. A tiny glitch is fine, but a freeze is not.

A second example is an **online game**. In a game, your position updates every 50 milliseconds. If one update is lost, the next update is coming anyway — resending the old position is useless. With TCP, later packets would wait until the lost packet was resent (head-of-line blocking), and the game would lag. With UDP, that never happens.

A third example is **DNS**. When you type a website name, one small DNS query goes out and one small answer comes back — the job is done in a single packet. A TCP handshake (3 packets) would be far too expensive for this. That is why DNS usually runs on UDP. If no answer comes, the app itself sends the query again — reliability sits with the app, not with the protocol.`
