        body: t(
          `**Galti 1: "UDP bhi reliable hai, bas tez hai" — nahi.** UDP mein koi reliability nahi hai. Agar aapko har byte sahi order mein chahiye, toh UDP galat choice hai. File download UDP par karoge toh file kharab ho jaayegi — ek packet kho gaya toh file ka hissa gayab. Isi liye file transfer, website aur email TCP par chalte hain.

**Galti 2: "UDP mein connection hota hai" — nahi.** UDP connectionless hai. \`connect\` naam ka function socket mein hota hai, par woh sirf local setting hai — network par koi handshake nahi hota. Do UDP app bas packet bhejti aur paati hain, bina pehle hello bole.

**Galti 3: UDP checksum ko optional samajhna.** UDP checksum se pata chalta hai ki packet raaste mein kharab hua ya nahi. IPv4 mein checksum optional hai, IPv6 mein compulsory. Kai log checksum ko band karke 2 byte bachane ki koshish karte hain, par kharab packet accept karne se app ka data bigad jaata hai. Checksum ko on rakhna chahiye.

**Galti 4: "UDP hamesha TCP se tez hai" — aadha sach.** Chhote aur time-sensitive kaam mein UDP tez hai. Par bade file transfer mein TCP aksar tez hota hai, kyunki woh congestion control se network ko jam hone se bachata hai aur khoye packet dobara bhejta hai. UDP bhar bhar ke bhejta rahe toh network jam ho jaata hai aur loss badh jaata hai.`,
          `**Mistake 1: "UDP is also reliable, just faster" — no.** UDP has no reliability at all. If you need every byte in the right order, UDP is the wrong choice. Download a file over UDP and the file will be damaged — one lost packet means a missing piece of the file. That is why file transfers, websites, and email run on TCP.

**Mistake 2: "UDP has a connection" — no.** UDP is connectionless. The socket has a function called \`connect\`, but that is only a local setting — no handshake happens on the network. Two UDP apps simply send and receive packets without saying hello first.

**Mistake 3: Treating the UDP checksum as optional.** The UDP checksum tells whether a packet was damaged on the way. It is optional in IPv4 and compulsory in IPv6. Some people turn the checksum off to save 2 bytes, but accepting a damaged packet corrupts the app's data. The checksum should stay on.

**Mistake 4: "UDP is always faster than TCP" — only half true.** UDP is faster for small, time-sensitive jobs. But for a large file transfer, TCP is often faster, because its congestion control stops the network from jamming and it resends lost packets. If UDP keeps flooding the network, the network jams and loss increases.`
        )
