          `**What if aapko UDP ki speed aur TCP ki reliability dono chahiye?** Yahi sawal Google ne poochha aur jawab banaya **QUIC** — **Quick UDP Internet Connections**. QUIC UDP ke upar chalta hai, par andar se TCP jaisi reliability deta hai: packet kho jaaye toh dobara bhejta hai, order theek karta hai, aur congestion control bhi karta hai. Aaj YouTube, Google aur Facebook ka bahut saara traffic QUIC par chalta hai, aur HTTP/3 QUIC ke upar hi bana hai.

QUIC ki sabse badi jeet hai **0-RTT connection**. TCP mein pehle handshake (1 RTT), phir security handshake (1 RTT), phir data — kul 2 RTT ka wait. QUIC mein pehli baar 1 RTT, aur dobara judne par **0 RTT** — pehle packet mein hi data bhej sakte ho. Mobile par network badalne (Wi-Fi se 4G) par bhi QUIC connection nahi tootta, kyunki woh connection ko IP se nahi, **connection ID** se pehchanta hai.

Doosri jeet hai **stream multiplexing bina head-of-line blocking ke**. TCP mein ek packet kho jaaye toh uske peeche ke saare packet ruk jaate hain, chahe woh alag file ke hon. QUIC mein har stream alag hoti hai — video wali stream ka packet kho jaaye toh sirf video rukta hai, chat wali stream chalti rehti hai. Neeche wala code yahi dikhata hai: 3 stream, ek mein loss, sirf 1 stream rukti hai.`,
          `**What if you want UDP's speed and TCP's reliability together?** Google asked this same question and built the answer: **QUIC** — **Quick UDP Internet Connections**. QUIC runs on top of UDP, but inside it gives TCP-like reliability: it resends lost packets, fixes the order, and does congestion control too. Today much of the traffic of YouTube, Google, and Facebook runs on QUIC, and HTTP/3 is built on top of QUIC.

The biggest win of QUIC is the **0-RTT connection**. In TCP, first the handshake (1 RTT), then the security handshake (1 RTT), then data — 2 RTTs of waiting in total. In QUIC, the first time takes 1 RTT, and on reconnecting it takes **0 RTT** — data can be sent in the very first packet. On mobile, when the network changes (Wi-Fi to 4G), the QUIC connection does not break, because it identifies the connection by a **connection ID**, not by IP.

The second win is **stream multiplexing without head-of-line blocking**. In TCP, if one packet is lost, every packet behind it waits, even if they belong to a different file. In QUIC, every stream is separate — if a packet of the video stream is lost, only the video waits, while the chat stream keeps running. The code below shows exactly this: 3 streams, a loss in one, and only 1 stream waits.`
