          `**UDP** is simple, connectionless, and unreliable — its header is only 8 bytes, with no handshake, no ACK, and no retransmission. That is why it is fast and wins at time-sensitive jobs: video calls, voice calls, online games, and DNS. Forget the lost packet, show the next one — that is the philosophy of UDP.

Things to remember: UDP does not fix order, does not remove duplicates, and a large datagram gets fragmented; if you need reliability, the app must add it itself or use TCP. **QUIC** builds reliability on top of UDP — a 0-RTT connection, a connection ID that survives mobile network changes, and separate streams that remove head-of-line blocking. HTTP/3 runs on QUIC.

In interviews, the most asked topics are: the difference between UDP and TCP, when to use UDP, and what QUIC is. If you can choose a protocol by asking "can I tolerate delay or loss?", you know UDP. In the next article we will look at **DNS** in depth — record types, recursive vs iterative, and caching.`
