          `**Socket** network par baat karne ka ek **endpoint** hai. Ek socket ko pehchanne ke liye do cheezein chahiye hoti hain: **IP address** (kaunsa computer) aur **port number** (us computer par kaunsi app). Jaise ghar ka pata IP hai aur kamre ka number port hai — sirf ghar ka pata jaanne se aap sahi kamre tak nahi pahunch sakte. Isi liye socket ko \`IP:port\` se likhte hain, jaise \`142.250.80.4:443\`.

**Port** 16-bit number hota hai, matlab 0 se 65535 tak. **Well-known port** 0 se 1023 tak hote hain aur bade service ke liye fix hote hain: 80 HTTP, 443 HTTPS, 53 DNS, 25 SMTP (email bhejna), 22 SSH. **Registered port** 1024 se 49151 tak hote hain, jaise 3306 MySQL. **Ephemeral port** 49152 se 65535 tak hote hain — client jab server se judta hai toh OS use ek temporary port deta hai, jo connection band hote hi wapas chala jaata hai.

Ek connection mein **do socket** hote hain — ek client ka, ek server ka. Dono mil kar ek **connection** banate hain, jise 4 cheezon se pehchana jaata hai: client IP, client port, server IP, server port. Isi liye ek hi server ke saath hazaaron client jud sakte hain — har client ka port alag hota hai, isliye server unhe alag pehchan leta hai.`,
          `A **socket** is an **endpoint** for talking on a network. To identify a socket you need two things: an **IP address** (which computer) and a **port number** (which app on that computer). Just as a house address is the IP and a room number is the port — knowing only the house address cannot take you to the right room. That is why a socket is written as \`IP:port\`, such as \`142.250.80.4:443\`.

A **port** is a 16-bit number, which means it runs from 0 to 65535. **Well-known ports** run from 0 to 1023 and are fixed for large services: 80 HTTP, 443 HTTPS, 53 DNS, 25 SMTP (sending email), 22 SSH. **Registered ports** run from 1024 to 49151, such as 3306 for MySQL. **Ephemeral ports** run from 49152 to 65535 — when a client connects to a server, the OS gives it a temporary port, which is returned as soon as the connection closes.

One connection has **two sockets** — one for the client and one for the server. Together they form a **connection**, identified by 4 things: client IP, client port, server IP, server port. That is why thousands of clients can connect to one server — every client has a different port, so the server can tell them apart.`
