          `Sabse seedha example **sheher mein rasta poochhna** hai. Aapko station jaana hai. Aap har chaurahe par khade aadmi se poochhte ho "station kidhar hai?" Woh kehta hai "seedha jao, 2 km hai" — yeh distance vector jaisa hai, har aadmi sirf apne aas-paas ka rasta jaanta hai aur aapko agla mod batata hai. Aapko poora map nahi pata, par har mod par sahi direction milti jaati hai.

Doosra example hai **metro map**. Aapke paas poora map hai — saari line, saare station, har do station ke beech ki doori. Aap khud hisaab laga kar sabse chhota rasta chun lete ho. Yeh link state jaisa hai — har router ke paas poori network ka map hota hai aur woh khud shortest path nikalta hai.

Teesra example hai **courier company**. Chhote sheher mein courier wala har parcel ko padosi sheher ke hub ko deta hai (distance vector — bas agla hub pata hai). Bade network mein company ke paas poora map hota hai aur woh parcel ko sabse tez route se bhejti hai (link state). Aur ek desh se doosre desh parcel bhejna **BGP** jaisa hai — har desh ki apni policy hoti hai ki parcel kis raste se jaaye.`,
          `The simplest example is **asking for directions in a city**. You want to go to the station. At every crossing you ask a person standing there, "which way is the station?" They say "go straight, it is 2 km" — this is like distance vector, where each person only knows the road around them and tells you the next turn. You never see the full map, but at every turn you get the right direction.

A second example is a **metro map**. You hold the full map — every line, every station, the distance between each pair of stations. You work out the shortest path yourself. This is like link state — every router holds a map of the whole network and computes the shortest path itself.

A third example is a **courier company**. In a small town, the courier hands every parcel to the next town's hub (distance vector — only the next hub is known). In a large network, the company holds the full map and sends the parcel by the fastest route (link state). And sending a parcel from one country to another is like **BGP** — every country has its own policy for which path the parcel takes.`
