          `Ab hum distance vector ko number ke saath dekhte hain. Network mein 4 router hain: A, B, C, D. Inke beech ke link aur unki cost hai: A-B = 1, A-C = 4, B-C = 2, B-D = 5, C-D = 1. Hum A se har router ki sabse chhoti doori nikalenge. Shuru mein sirf A ki doori 0 pata hai, baaki sab ko 99 (matlab anjaan) maan lete hain.

Distance vector ka rule hai **Bellman-Ford**: har link ko dekho aur poochho — "is link se jaane par doori kam hoti hai kya?" Agar haan, toh doori update karo. Yeh kaam tab tak doh rao jab tak koi doori badle hi nahi.

**Round 1:** A-B (1) se B = 1. A-C (4) se C = 4. B-C (2) se C = 1 + 2 = 3, jo 4 se kam hai — C = 3. B-D (5) se D = 1 + 5 = 6. C-D (1) se D = 3 + 1 = 4, jo 6 se kam hai — D = 4. Round 1 ke baad: A = 0, B = 1, C = 3, D = 4.

**Round 2 aur 3:** Koi bhi link doori kam nahi karta — sab stable hai. Matlab table **converge** ho gayi hai. A se D ka sabse chhota rasta hai A-B-C-D, cost 1 + 2 + 1 = 4. Seedha A-C-D hota toh cost 4 + 1 = 5 hoti — isliye router B se hokar jaata hai. Neeche wala code yahi 3 round chalata hai aur har round ke baad table print karta hai.`,
          `Now let us see distance vector with numbers. The network has 4 routers: A, B, C, D. The links between them and their costs are: A-B = 1, A-C = 4, B-C = 2, B-D = 5, C-D = 1. We will find the shortest distance from A to every router. At the start, only A's distance 0 is known; the rest are taken as 99 (meaning unknown).

The distance vector rule is **Bellman-Ford**: look at every link and ask — "does going through this link make the distance smaller?" If yes, update the distance. Repeat this until no distance changes at all.

**Round 1:** A-B (1) gives B = 1. A-C (4) gives C = 4. B-C (2) gives C = 1 + 2 = 3, which is less than 4 — so C = 3. B-D (5) gives D = 1 + 5 = 6. C-D (1) gives D = 3 + 1 = 4, which is less than 6 — so D = 4. After round 1: A = 0, B = 1, C = 3, D = 4.

**Rounds 2 and 3:** No link reduces any distance — everything is stable. That means the table has **converged**. The shortest path from A to D is A-B-C-D, with cost 1 + 2 + 1 = 4. Going straight A-C-D would cost 4 + 1 = 5 — that is why the router goes through B. The code below runs these 3 rounds and prints the table after each round.`
