          `**Galti 1: Count to infinity ko ignore karna.** Distance vector mein agar ek link toot jaaye, toh do router ek doosre ko galat doori batate rehte hain — "mera rasta 2 hai", "mera 3 hai", "mera 4 hai" — aur number badhta hi rehta hai. Ise **count to infinity** kehte hain. Isse rokne ke liye **infinity ko 16** maan lete hain (RIP mein) — 16 ka matlab hai "pahuncha nahi ja sakta." Isi liye RIP mein 15 hop se zyada ka rasta unreachable mana jaata hai.

**Galti 2: RIP aur OSPF ko ek samajhna.** **RIP** distance vector hai, sirf hop count dekhta hai, har 30 second mein poori table bhejta hai, aur 15 hop se zyada nahi ja sakta. **OSPF** link state hai, bandwidth aur delay jaisi cost dekhta hai, sirf change hone par update bhejta hai, aur bade network mein chalta hai. RIP chhote network ke liye hai, OSPF bade ke liye. Interview mein yeh farak seedha poochha jaata hai.

**Galti 3: "Sabse chhota rasta hamesha sabse tez hai" — nahi.** RIP sirf hop count dekhta hai. 2 hop wala rasta slow link se ja sakta hai, jabki 3 hop wala rasta fast fibre se ja sakta hai. RIP phir bhi 2 hop wala chunega. OSPF cost mein bandwidth ko bhi dekhta hai, isliye woh tez rasta chunta hai. Isi liye bade network mein OSPF behtar hai.

**Galti 4: BGP ko andar ka protocol samajhna.** **BGP** network ke andar kaam nahi karta — woh **autonomous system (AS)** ke beech kaam karta hai, jaise ek ISP se doosre ISP tak. BGP shortest path nahi, **policy** dekhta hai — kaunsa rasta sasta hai, kis se agreement hai. Isi liye internet ka routing policy se chalta hai, sirf doori se nahi.`,
          `**Mistake 1: Ignoring count to infinity.** In distance vector, if a link breaks, two routers keep telling each other wrong distances — "my path is 2", "mine is 3", "mine is 4" — and the number keeps growing. This is called **count to infinity**. To stop it, **infinity is taken as 16** (in RIP) — 16 means "unreachable". That is why in RIP a path of more than 15 hops counts as unreachable.

**Mistake 2: Treating RIP and OSPF as the same.** **RIP** is distance vector, it looks only at hop count, sends its whole table every 30 seconds, and cannot go beyond 15 hops. **OSPF** is link state, it looks at costs such as bandwidth and delay, sends updates only when something changes, and runs in large networks. RIP is for small networks, OSPF for large ones. Interviews ask directly about this difference.

**Mistake 3: "The shortest path is always the fastest" — no.** RIP looks only at hop count. A 2-hop path may go over a slow link, while a 3-hop path may go over fast fibre. RIP still picks the 2-hop path. OSPF also looks at bandwidth in its cost, so it picks the faster path. That is why OSPF is better in large networks.

**Mistake 4: Treating BGP as an inside protocol.** **BGP** does not work inside a network — it works **between autonomous systems (AS)**, such as from one ISP to another ISP. BGP does not look for the shortest path; it looks at **policy** — which path is cheaper and who has an agreement with whom. That is why internet routing runs on policy, not only on distance.`
