          `**Link state kaise kaam karta hai?** Har router apne padosi link ki cost naap kar poori network mein **flood** karta hai — "mera B se link cost 1 hai, C se 4 hai." Har router ko sabki khabar mil jaati hai aur woh poora map bana leta hai. Phir woh **Dijkstra algorithm** chalata hai aur har destination ka shortest path nikal leta hai. Distance vector mein router sirf padosi se table leta hai; link state mein router khud map se hisaab lagata hai. Neeche wala code Dijkstra se wahi network solve karta hai jo upar Bellman-Ford se solve kiya tha — jawab wahi aata hai: A se D = 4.

**What if poora internet ek hi protocol par chalta?** Namumkin hai. Ek ISP apni andar ki topology doosre ISP ko nahi batana chahta — woh business secret hai. Isi liye andar OSPF (link state) aur bahar BGP (policy) chalta hai. BGP mein har ISP apni policy lagata hai: "is rasta se traffic lo, us se nahi."

**What if router ko cost ki parwah nahi hoti?** Toh packet kisi bhi raste par chala jaata — kabhi lamba rasta, kabhi jam wala rasta. Routing ka poora matlab hi cost se sahi rasta chunna hai. Cost hop count ho sakta hai, delay ho sakta hai, ya bandwidth — protocol ke hisaab se.`,
          `**How does link state work?** Every router measures the cost of its neighbour links and **floods** it across the whole network — "my link to B costs 1, to C it costs 4." Every router receives everybody's news and builds the full map. Then it runs the **Dijkstra algorithm** and computes the shortest path to every destination. In distance vector, a router only takes a table from its neighbour; in link state, the router computes from the map itself. The code below solves the same network with Dijkstra that Bellman-Ford solved above — the answer is the same: A to D = 4.

**What if the whole internet ran on one protocol?** That is impossible. One ISP does not want to tell another ISP its inside topology — that is a business secret. That is why OSPF (link state) runs inside, and BGP (policy) runs outside. In BGP, every ISP applies its own policy: "take traffic through this path, not that one."

**What if a router did not care about cost?** Then a packet would take any path — sometimes a long one, sometimes a jammed one. The whole point of routing is picking the right path by cost. Cost can be hop count, delay, or bandwidth — depending on the protocol.`
