          `**Switching** ka matlab hai data ko ek network se doosre network tak pahunchane ka tareeka. Jab aapka message Delhi se Mumbai jaata hai, toh beech mein kai switch aur router padte hain. Har switch ko decide karna padta hai ki message ko aage kis line par bhejna hai. Is decision ke teen tareeke hain: **circuit switching**, **packet switching** aur **message switching**.

**Circuit switching** mein pehle poora rasta book hota hai, phir baat hoti hai — jaise purane telephone mein operator taar jodta tha. Rasta book hone ke baad woh sirf aapka hota hai, koi aur use nahi kar sakta. Baat khatam hone par rasta chhod diya jaata hai.

**Packet switching** mein message ko chhote-chhote **packet** mein tod diya jaata hai. Har packet alag-alag raste se ja sakta hai aur destination par phir se jud jaata hai. Internet isi par chalta hai. Rasta book nahi hota — jo line khaali hai, packet usi se nikal jaata hai.

**Message switching** mein poora message ek saath ek node se doosre node tak jaata hai. Har node poora message paata hai, store karta hai, phir aage bhejta hai. Yeh purana tareeka hai — aaj kal kam use hota hai, par iska idea (store-and-forward) packet switching mein bhi hai.`,
          `**Switching** means the way data is carried from one network to another. When your message travels from Delhi to Mumbai, it passes through many switches and routers. Each switch must decide which line to send the message on next. There are three ways to make this decision: **circuit switching**, **packet switching**, and **message switching**.

In **circuit switching**, the whole path is booked first, and then the talk happens — like the old telephone, where the operator joined the wires. Once the path is booked, it is yours alone; nobody else can use it. When the talk ends, the path is released.

In **packet switching**, the message is broken into small **packets**. Each packet can travel by a different path and they join up again at the destination. The internet runs on this. No path is booked — a packet takes whichever line is free.

In **message switching**, the whole message travels at once from one node to the next. Each node receives the full message, stores it, and then sends it onward. This is an old method — rarely used today, but its idea (store-and-forward) also lives inside packet switching.`
