          `Ek **network** ka matlab hai do ya zyada devices ka judna taaki woh data share kar sakein. Par network ka naam uske **size** se banta hai. **LAN (Local Area Network)** ek chhoti jagah ka network hai — ek ghar, ek office floor, ya ek lab. Tumhara ghar ka WiFi ek LAN hai: phone, laptop aur smart TV ek hi router se jude hain. LAN fast hota hai, sasta hota hai, aur ek hi owner ke control mein rehta hai.

**MAN (Metropolitan Area Network)** ek sheher ke size ka network hai. Socho ek sheher ke saare cable TV offices ya ek university ke alag-alag campus ek hi network se jude hon — woh MAN hai. Yeh LAN se bada aur WAN se chhota hota hai. **WAN (Wide Area Network)** sabse bada hota hai — ek desh, ya poori duniya. Internet khud duniya ka sabse bada WAN hai, aur ek bank ki Mumbai, Delhi aur London branches ko jodne wala network bhi WAN hai. WAN mein data lambe distance par travel karta hai, isliye woh LAN se slow aur mehenga hota hai.

In teeno ke beech ek chhota bhai bhi hai: **PAN (Personal Area Network)**, jaise phone se Bluetooth earphones ka connection — sirf 1-2 metre ka range. Interview mein aksar poochha jaata hai: "LAN aur WAN mein farak kya hai?" Answer hamesha teen points mein do — **size, speed aur ownership**. LAN chhota, fast aur private hota hai; WAN bada, slow aur aksar rented lines (jaise leased line ya internet) par chalta hai. MAN beech mein baithta hai.`,
          `A **network** means two or more devices connected so they can share data. But a network gets its name from its **size**. A **LAN (Local Area Network)** is a network for one small place — a home, one office floor, or a lab. Your home WiFi is a LAN: your phone, laptop, and smart TV all connect to the same router. A LAN is fast, cheap, and under one owner's control.

A **MAN (Metropolitan Area Network)** covers a whole city. Imagine all the cable TV offices in a city, or the separate campuses of one university, joined into a single network — that is a MAN. It is bigger than a LAN and smaller than a WAN. A **WAN (Wide Area Network)** is the biggest of all — a country, or the whole world. The internet itself is the world's biggest WAN, and a bank's network joining its Mumbai, Delhi, and London branches is also a WAN. In a WAN, data travels over long distances, so it is slower and more expensive than a LAN.

There is also a smaller sibling: a **PAN (Personal Area Network)**, like the Bluetooth link between your phone and your earphones — a range of only 1 or 2 metres. Interviews often ask: "What is the difference between a LAN and a WAN?" Always answer in three points — **size, speed, and ownership**. A LAN is small, fast, and private; a WAN is large, slow, and usually runs on rented lines (like a leased line or the internet). A MAN sits in between.`
