          `Sabse pehla example tumhare ghar mein hi hai. Tumhara **home WiFi ek LAN** hai. Router ke saath 5-6 devices jude hain, aur ek device se doosre device par photo bhejna ya ek hi printer share karna LAN ka kaam hai. Ghar se bahar nikalte hi phone mobile data par chala jaata hai — woh ek WAN (mobile operator ka network) se jud jaata hai, isliye speed aur cost dono badal jaate hain.

Doosra example hai **college campus**. Ek college ke alag-alag buildings — library, hostel, labs — mein alag-alag LANs hote hain, aur woh sab ek campus network se jude hote hain. Poora campus ek sheher ke hisse mein ho toh use MAN bhi keh sakte hain. Student ko yeh dikhai nahi deta, par jab woh hostel se library ke server par file kholta hai, toh woh campus network par travel kar rahi hoti hai.

Teesra example hai **bank ya company ka WAN**. Ek bank ki har branch mein apna LAN hota hai, aur saari branches ek WAN se head office se judi hoti hain. Jab tum ATM se paise nikaalte ho, toh request tumhari branch ke LAN se nikal kar WAN par travel karke central server tak jaati hai aur wapas aati hai. Isi liye ATM transaction mein 2-3 second lagte hain, jabki ghar ke LAN par file transfer turant ho jaata hai. Yahi size ka asar hai: distance badhti hai, toh delay badhta hai.`,
          `The first example is right in your home. Your **home WiFi is a LAN**. Five or six devices connect to the router, and sending a photo from one device to another or sharing one printer is the LAN's job. The moment you step outside, your phone switches to mobile data — it joins a WAN (the mobile operator's network), which is why both the speed and the cost change.

The second example is a **college campus**. Each building — the library, the hostel, the labs — has its own LAN, and all of them join one campus network. If the whole campus sits inside one city, you can also call it a MAN. A student never sees this, but when they open a file on the library server from the hostel, that file travels across the campus network.

The third example is a **bank or company WAN**. Every branch of a bank has its own LAN, and all branches join the head office over a WAN. When you withdraw money from an ATM, the request leaves your branch's LAN, travels across the WAN to the central server, and comes back. That is why an ATM transaction takes 2 or 3 seconds, while a file transfer on your home LAN feels instant. That is the effect of size: as distance grows, delay grows.`
