        body: t(
          `LAN/WAN aur topology mein chaar galtiyan baar-baar hoti hain.

**Galti 1: Topology ko physical map samajh lena.** Star topology ka matlab yeh nahi hai ki computers kamre mein taara (star) shape mein rakhe hain. Topology **connection ka pattern** hai, furniture ki layout nahi. Saare computers ek hi line mein rakhe hon, phir bhi unka connection star ho sakta hai kyunki sab ek switch se jude hain. Interview mein "logical topology" aur "physical topology" ka farak poochha jaata hai — logical matlab data kis raaste jaata hai, physical matlab cables kahan se kahan jaati hain.

**Galti 2: Mesh ko hamesha best samajhna.** Mesh sabse reliable hai, par har jagah best nahi hai. 5 devices ke liye 10 cables theek hain, par 50 devices ke liye mesh mein 1,225 cables lagenge. Cost aur maintenance dono impossible ho jaate hain. Isliye real offices star use karte hain aur mesh sirf wahan use hota hai jahan failure bilkul allowed nahi hai, jaise bank ke core routers ke beech.

**Galti 3: VLAN ko alag physical network samajhna.** **VLAN (Virtual LAN)** ek hi physical switch ko logical hisson mein baant deta hai. Ek switch par VLAN 10 mein accounts team aur VLAN 20 mein HR team ho sakti hai — dono ek hi switch par hain, par ek doosre se baat nahi kar sakti. Yeh alag cables ka kaam nahi hai, yeh switch ki setting ka kaam hai. VLAN ka fayda hai security aur broadcast traffic kam karna.

**Galti 4: LAN aur internet ko ek samajhna.** LAN sirf tumhara local network hai. Internet ek WAN hai. Jab tum ghar ke WiFi par ho aur internet band ho jaaye, toh bhi phone se laptop par file bhej sakte ho — LAN chal raha hai, WAN band hai. Dono ko alag samajhna zaroori hai.`,
          `Four mistakes come up again and again with LAN/WAN and topology.

**Mistake 1: Treating a topology as a physical map.** A star topology does not mean the computers sit in a star shape in the room. A topology is the **pattern of connections**, not the furniture layout. All the computers can sit in one straight line and still form a star, because they all connect to one switch. Interviews ask about the difference between "logical topology" and "physical topology" — logical means the path data takes, physical means where the cables actually run.

**Mistake 2: Thinking a mesh is always best.** A mesh is the most reliable, but it is not best everywhere. Ten cables for 5 devices is fine, but for 50 devices a mesh needs 1,225 cables. The cost and the maintenance both become impossible. That is why real offices use a star, and a mesh is used only where failure is not allowed at all, such as between a bank's core routers.

**Mistake 3: Thinking a VLAN is a separate physical network.** A **VLAN (Virtual LAN)** splits one physical switch into logical parts. One switch can hold the accounts team on VLAN 10 and the HR team on VLAN 20 — both sit on the same switch, but they cannot talk to each other. That is not the work of separate cables; it is a setting on the switch. The benefit of a VLAN is security and less broadcast traffic.

**Mistake 4: Treating a LAN and the internet as the same thing.** A LAN is only your local network. The internet is a WAN. When you are on home WiFi and the internet goes down, you can still send a file from your phone to your laptop — the LAN is running while the WAN is down. You must keep the two separate in your mind.`
        )
