          `**Wireless network** mein devices bina cable ke **radio waves** se jude hote hain. Tumhara phone WiFi router se ek invisible radio link se juda hai — koi taar nahi, phir bhi data aata-jaata hai. Iska standard hai **IEEE 802.11**, jise hum aam bol-chaal mein **WiFi** kehte hain. 802.11 ek family hai: 802.11b, 802.11g, 802.11n, 802.11ac aur 802.11ax (WiFi 6) — har nayi generation zyada speed aur zyada devices handle karti hai.

WiFi do frequency bands par chalta hai: **2.4 GHz** aur **5 GHz**. 2.4 GHz band door tak jaata hai aur deewaar ko behtar paar karta hai, par speed kam hoti hai aur bheed zyada hoti hai (microwave aur Bluetooth bhi yahi band use karte hain). 5 GHz band fast hota hai par range chhoti hoti hai — do kamre door jaate hi signal kamzor ho jaata hai. Isi liye router ke paas 5 GHz fast lagta hai aur door wale kamre mein 2.4 GHz zyada stable rehta hai.

WiFi mein ek **access point (AP)** hota hai — tumhara router hi AP hai. Saare devices AP se judte hain aur AP unke beech data forward karta hai. Yeh star topology ka wireless version hai: centre mein AP, aas-paas devices. Jab tum ek naye WiFi se judte ho, toh pehle **SSID** (network ka naam) dikhta hai, phir password se **authentication** hoti hai, aur phir device ko ek IP address milta hai.`,
          `In a **wireless network**, devices connect with **radio waves** instead of cables. Your phone joins the WiFi router over an invisible radio link — no wire, yet data flows both ways. The standard behind it is **IEEE 802.11**, which we call **WiFi** in everyday language. 802.11 is a family: 802.11b, 802.11g, 802.11n, 802.11ac, and 802.11ax (WiFi 6) — each new generation brings more speed and handles more devices.

WiFi runs on two frequency bands: **2.4 GHz** and **5 GHz**. The 2.4 GHz band reaches further and passes through walls better, but it is slower and more crowded (microwaves and Bluetooth use the same band). The 5 GHz band is faster but has a shorter range — two rooms away and the signal is already weak. That is why 5 GHz feels fast near the router, while 2.4 GHz stays more stable in the far room.

WiFi has an **access point (AP)** — your router is the AP. All devices connect to the AP, and the AP forwards data between them. This is the wireless version of a star topology: the AP in the centre, devices around it. When you join a new WiFi network, you first see the **SSID** (the network name), then **authentication** happens with the password, and then the device gets an IP address.`
