          `Edge cases mein wireless ki asli mushkilein dikhti hain.

**1. Lift ya basement mein signal gayab.** Metal aur concrete radio waves ko rokte hain. Lift ek metal box hai, isliye andar jaate hi mobile signal gayab ho jaata hai. Yahi wajah hai ki basement mein WiFi router ke paas bhi signal kamzor ho jaata hai — deewaar aur floor waves ko absorb kar lete hain.

**2. Bheed wala hotspot.** Ek access point se 100 users jud jaayein toh har user ko thoda-thoda time slot milta hai. WiFi mein devices baari-baari bolte hain (pehle suno, phir bolo), isliye bheed mein har device ko wait karna padta hai. Yeh wired network se bilkul alag hai, jahan har device ki apni cable hoti hai.

**3. Bluetooth pairing fail hona.** Bluetooth mein pehle **pairing** hoti hai — dono devices ek doosre ko pehchan kar ek shared key banate hain. Agar ek device pehle se kisi aur se paired ho, ya range 10 metre se zyada ho, toh pairing fail hoti hai. Bluetooth ka range chhota isliye hai kyunki woh bahut kam power use karta hai — earphones ki battery bachane ke liye.

**4. 4G se 5G par switch.** 5G ke towers kam hain, isliye phone 5G area se bahar nikalte hi 4G par wapas aa jaata hai. Yeh switch automatic hota hai, par us ek second mein video ruk sakta hai. 5G ki sabse badi taakat sirf speed nahi hai, **kam latency** bhi hai — 4G mein lagbhag 50 ms, 5G mein 10 ms ke aas-paas.`,
          `Edge cases show the real difficulties of wireless networks.

**1. Signal disappears in a lift.** Metal and concrete block radio waves. A lift is a metal box, so the mobile signal vanishes the moment you step inside. That is also why WiFi gets weak in a basement even near the router — walls and floors absorb the waves.

**2. A crowded hotspot.** When 100 users join one access point, each user gets a small time slot. In WiFi, devices take turns (listen first, then speak), so in a crowd every device must wait. This is completely different from a wired network, where every device has its own cable.

**3. Bluetooth pairing fails.** In Bluetooth, **pairing** comes first — the two devices recognise each other and create a shared key. If one device is already paired with another, or the range is more than 10 metres, pairing fails. Bluetooth range is short because it uses very little power — to save the earphones' battery.

**4. Switching from 4G to 5G.** 5G towers are still few, so the moment a phone leaves a 5G area it falls back to 4G. The switch is automatic, but a video can pause for that one second. The biggest strength of 5G is not only speed but also **low latency** — about 50 ms on 4G, and around 10 ms on 5G.`
