          `Edge cases mein protocols ki limits dikhti hain.

**1. Bada attachment bheja toh?** SMTP ke through bada attachment bhejne par mail server use reject kar sakta hai — aam taur par 25 MB ki limit hoti hai. Isi liye badi files mail se nahi, link se bheji jaati hain (jaise Drive link). Attachment ko base64 mein encode kiya jaata hai, jisse size lagbhag 33% badh jaata hai — 20 MB ki file mail mein 27 MB ki ho jaati hai.

**2. Receiver ka server down ho toh?** SMTP mein sender ka server mail ko **queue** mein rakh leta hai aur thodi-thodi der mein dobara try karta hai. Agar 2-3 din tak server up nahi aaya, toh sender ko bounce mail milta hai — "delivery failed". Email isi liye reliable hai: bhejne wala turant fail nahi hota, system retry karta hai.

**3. Do devices par mail padha toh?** IMAP par phone aur laptop dono server se sync hote hain — ek par delete kiya toh doosre par bhi delete dikhega. POP3 par aisa nahi hota: phone par download kiya toh laptop par mail hi nahi milega, kyunki server se delete ho gaya. Yahi farak aaj har user ko samajhna chahiye.

**4. FTP mein username/password plain text mein?** Haan, normal FTP mein bhi login plain text mein jaata hai — isiliye aaj **SFTP** (FTP over SSH, port 22) ya **FTPS** (FTP over TLS) use hota hai.`,
          `Edge cases show the limits of these protocols.

**1. What if you send a big attachment?** A mail server can reject a big attachment sent over SMTP — the usual limit is 25 MB. That is why big files are sent as a link (like a Drive link) instead of an attachment. An attachment is encoded in base64, which grows its size by about 33% — a 20 MB file becomes about 27 MB inside the mail.

**2. What if the receiver's server is down?** In SMTP, the sender's server keeps the mail in a **queue** and retries every so often. If the server does not come up for 2 or 3 days, the sender gets a bounce mail — "delivery failed". That is why email is reliable: the sender does not fail at once; the system retries.

**3. What if you read mail on two devices?** With IMAP, the phone and the laptop both sync with the server — delete on one and it shows deleted on the other. With POP3 this does not happen: download on the phone and the laptop will never see the mail, because it was deleted from the server. Every user today should understand this difference.

**4. Does FTP send the username and password in plain text?** Yes, in plain FTP the login also travels in plain text — which is why today **SFTP** (FTP over SSH, port 22) or **FTPS** (FTP over TLS) is used.`
