          `**Network attack** ka matlab hai kisi network ya system ko nuksaan pahunchane, band karne, ya data churane ki koshish. Attacker ka goal hamesha teen mein se ek hota hai: **service band karna**, **data chori karna**, ya **galat pehchan se dhokha dena**. Chaar attacks ko samajh lo, toh baaki sab inhi ke variations hain.

**DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service)** mein attacker hazaron computers se ek server par itni requests bhejta hai ki server asli users ko jawab hi nahi de pata — jaise ek dukaan ke darwaze par itni bheed laga do ki asli customer andar hi na aa sake. **MITM (Man-in-the-Middle)** mein attacker tum aur server ke beech mein baith kar tumhara data padhta hai ya badal deta hai — jaise postman tumhara letter khol kar padh le.

**Phishing** mein attacker tumhe fake email ya fake website se dhokha dekar tumhara password ya OTP maangta hai — jaise koi bank ka naam lekar tumhe call kare aur kahe "aapka account band ho raha hai, OTP batao". **Spoofing** mein attacker apni pehchan badal leta hai — fake IP address, fake email address, ya fake website se aisa dikhata hai jaise woh koi aur ho.`,
          `A **network attack** means trying to harm a network or system, shut it down, or steal data. An attacker's goal is always one of three things: **stop a service**, **steal data**, or **deceive with a false identity**. If you understand four attacks, the rest are just variations of them.

In a **DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service)** attack, the attacker sends so many requests to a server from thousands of computers that the server cannot answer real users at all — like crowding a shop's door so thick that real customers cannot get inside. In a **MITM (Man-in-the-Middle)** attack, the attacker sits between you and the server and reads or changes your data — like a postman opening and reading your letter.

In **phishing**, the attacker tricks you with a fake email or fake website into giving your password or OTP — like someone calling in a bank's name and saying "your account is closing, tell me the OTP". In **spoofing**, the attacker changes their identity — with a fake IP address, a fake email address, or a fake website, pretending to be someone else.`
