          `Pehla example hai **ghar ka router**. Tumhara ghar ka "router" asal mein teen devices ka combination hai: modem (internet line se judta hai), router (tumhare LAN aur internet ke beech IP routing karta hai) aur switch + WiFi access point (ghar ke devices ko jodta hai). Isi liye ek hi box mein itne saare kaam hote hain — modem, router, switch aur access point sab ek saath.

Doosra example hai **office ka switch**. Office mein 50 computers ek switch se jude hote hain. Jab computer A computer B ko file bhejta hai, toh switch sirf B wale port par frame bhejta hai — baaki 48 computers ko pata bhi nahi chalta. Isi liye office network fast aur private rehta hai. Agar yahan hub hota, toh har file har computer tak jaati aur network slow ho jaata.

Teesra example hai **repeater**. Ghar mein ek kamre mein WiFi signal kamzor hai, toh repeater lagaya jaata hai — woh router ka signal pakad kar use dobara strong karke aage badhata hai. Repeater signal ko samajhta nahi hai, sirf amplify karta hai — jaise awaaz ko loudspeaker se zor se bolna.`,
          `The first example is your **home router**. Your home "router" is actually a combination of three devices: a modem (connects to the internet line), a router (does IP routing between your LAN and the internet), and a switch plus WiFi access point (joins the home devices). That is why one box does so many jobs — modem, router, switch, and access point all together.

The second example is an **office switch**. Fifty computers in an office connect to one switch. When computer A sends a file to computer B, the switch sends the frame only to B's port — the other 48 computers never even know. That is why the office network stays fast and private. If a hub were here instead, every file would go to every computer and the network would slow down.

The third example is a **repeater**. If the WiFi signal is weak in one room at home, a repeater is installed — it catches the router's signal and pushes it forward, strong again. A repeater does not understand the signal; it only amplifies it — like speaking a voice louder through a loudspeaker.`
