          `Ab numbers ke saath kaam karte hain. **FDM wala hissa:** maan lo hamare paas 100 MHz ka band hai. Har channel ko 5 MHz chahiye, aur do channels ke beech mein 1 MHz ki guard band chahiye. Toh har channel asal mein 5 + 1 = 6 MHz jagah leta hai. Kitne channels banenge? 100 // 6 = 16 channels. 16 channels mein 16 x 6 = 96 MHz use hoga, aur 100 - 96 = 4 MHz bekaar reh jaayega. Yeh bekaar jagah guard band aur rounding ki wajah se banti hai — yeh FDM ki keemat hai.

**TDM wala hissa:** 4 users — A, B, C, D — ek hi link share kar rahe hain. Har user ko 1 ms ka slot milta hai. Round 1 mein order hai A, B, C, D. Round 2 mein phir wahi order. Har round 4 ms ka hota hai, isliye har user ko har 4 ms mein 1 ms milta hai, matlab har user ko link ka 1/4 hissa milta hai. A ko apni baari ke beech mein 3 ms intezaar karna padta hai — B, C aur D ke slots ka time.

Ab dono ko compare karo: FDM mein har user ko hamesha apni frequency milti hai, par band fixed hai — user kam ho toh bhi jagah khaali rehti hai. TDM mein time baant-ta hai, isliye agar B ke paas bhejne ko kuch nahi hai, toh uska slot bekaar chala jaata hai, jab tak system slot ko dobara na baant de. Aadhunik system isi liye **statistical TDM** use karte hain: slot sirf un users ko milta hai jinke paas data hai. Neeche wala code dono hisaab print karta hai — isko line-by-line padho aur numbers ko haath se bhi check karo.`,
          `Now let us work with numbers. **The FDM part:** suppose we have a 100 MHz band. Each channel needs 5 MHz, and a 1 MHz guard band is needed between two channels. So each channel really takes 5 + 1 = 6 MHz of space. How many channels fit? 100 // 6 = 16 channels. Sixteen channels use 16 x 6 = 96 MHz, and 100 - 96 = 4 MHz is left unused. That wasted space comes from the guard bands and rounding — it is the price of FDM.

**The TDM part:** 4 users — A, B, C, and D — share one link. Every user gets a 1 ms slot. In round 1 the order is A, B, C, D. In round 2 the same order repeats. Each round lasts 4 ms, so every user gets 1 ms out of every 4 ms, which means each user gets one quarter of the link. A must wait 3 ms between turns — the time of the B, C, and D slots.

Now compare the two: in FDM every user always has its own frequency, but the band is fixed — even if there are fewer users, the space stays empty. In TDM the time is shared, so if B has nothing to send, its slot is wasted unless the system gives the slot to someone else. That is why modern systems use **statistical TDM**: slots go only to users who actually have data. The code below prints both calculations — read it line by line and check the numbers by hand as well.`
