          `The most common multiplexing mistakes happen in three places.

**Mistake 1: Leaving the guard band out of the calculation.** Beginners divide 100 MHz straight by 5 MHz and say 20 channels fit. Wrong! Each channel also takes a 1 MHz guard band, so every channel is 6 MHz wide and only 16 channels fit. Exams always test the guard band — if the question gives one, you must add it. Forgetting the guard band means an answer that is wrong by 4 channels.

**Mistake 2: Mixing up FDM and TDM.** In FDM the frequency is split and everyone speaks at the same time; in TDM the time is split and everyone speaks in turn. People often say "in TDM each user gets a different frequency" — no, in TDM everyone uses the same frequency and only the time differs. A way to remember: **F**DM splits **F**requency, **T**DM splits **T**ime.

**Mistake 3: Thinking CDMA is encryption.** The code in CDMA is not a secret; it is an identity. The code does not hide the data, it separates the users. Encryption is the job of a different layer. If you describe CDMA as a security method in an interview, that is wrong — CDMA is a sharing method, not a security method.

**Mistake 4: Assuming every user gets equal bandwidth in TDM.** In fixed TDM, yes, every user gets an equal slot. But if one user has nothing to send, its slot is wasted and the other users gain nothing from it. That is why modern networks use statistical TDM, where slots go only to busy users.`
