          `Edge cases woh situations hain jo normal hisaab mein nahi aati, par exam aur interview mein yahi poochhi jaati hain.

**1. Khaali slot (idle user).** TDM mein agar B ke paas bhejne ko kuch nahi hai, toh bhi uska slot aata hai aur khaali chala jaata hai. Link ka 1/4 hissa bekaar ho jaata hai. Statistical TDM mein aisa nahi hota — slot sirf unko milta hai jinke paas data hai. Isi liye data network mein statistical TDM zyada popular hai, jabki purani telephone lines mein fixed TDM chalta tha, kyunki wahan har user lagbhag hamesha bolta rehta tha.

**2. Guard band zero.** Agar guard band 0 ho, toh channels ek-doosre se takraane lagenge — isse **interference** kehte hain. Real system mein guard band kabhi zero nahi hota, kyunki filter perfect nahi hote aur signal thoda sa fail jaata hai. Exam mein agar guard band 0 diya ho, toh hisaab simple ho jaata hai: channels = band // channel width. Par yaad rakho, yeh sirf theory hai, real life mein aisa nahi chalta.

**3. Sirf ek user.** Agar ek hi user ho, toh multiplexing ki zaroorat hi nahi hai — poora band ya poora time usi ka hai. FDM mein ek channel poora band le lega, TDM mein har slot usi ka hoga. Yeh edge case samajhna isliye zaroori hai kyunki yeh dikhata hai ki multiplexing sirf sharing ke liye hai, speed badhane ke liye nahi. Ek user ki speed multiplexing se nahi badhti, woh toh medium ki limit se bandhi hoti hai.

**4. Bahut zyada users.** Agar users badh jaayein toh FDM mein channels khatam ho jaate hain aur TDM mein har user ki baari der se aati hai — delay badh jaata hai. Isi liye mobile network mein users badhne par tower par load badhta hai aur call quality girti hai. Solution hai aur band lena, chhote cells banana ya behtar code use karna.`,
          `Edge cases are the situations that never appear in a normal calculation, but exams and interviews ask about exactly these.

**1. Empty slot (idle user).** In TDM, if B has nothing to send, its slot still comes and goes empty. One quarter of the link is wasted. Statistical TDM avoids this — slots go only to users who have data. That is why statistical TDM is more popular in data networks, while old telephone lines used fixed TDM, because there every user was almost always talking.

**2. Zero guard band.** If the guard band is 0, channels start colliding with each other — this is called **interference**. A real system never has a zero guard band, because filters are not perfect and signals spread a little. If an exam gives a guard band of 0, the calculation becomes simple: channels = band // channel width. But remember, that is only theory; it does not work in real life.

**3. Only one user.** If there is just one user, multiplexing is not needed at all — the whole band or the whole time belongs to that user. In FDM one channel takes the whole band, and in TDM every slot is theirs. This edge case matters because it shows that multiplexing exists only for sharing, not for speed. One user's speed does not increase with multiplexing; it is limited by the medium itself.

**4. Too many users.** If users grow in number, FDM runs out of channels and in TDM every user's turn comes late — delay grows. That is why, as users increase on a mobile network, the tower gets loaded and call quality drops. The fix is to take more band, build smaller cells, or use better codes.`
