          `IP addressing mein sabse zyada galtiyan chaar cheezon par hoti hain.

**Galti 1: Network ya broadcast address ko device ko dena.** \`192.168.1.0\` aur \`192.168.1.255\` kisi device ko nahi diye ja sakte — pehla poore network ki pehchan hai, doosra sabko message bhejne ke liye hai. Beginners aksar .0 ko pehla host samajh lete hain. Pehla usable host hamesha .1 hota hai, .0 nahi. Agar aap .0 kisi laptop ko de doge, toh network kaam nahi karega.

**Galti 2: Usable hosts ginne mein 2 bhoolna.** /24 mein total 256 addresses hain, par usable 254 hain, 256 nahi. Har baar 2 ghatao — network aur broadcast. GATE mein yeh sabse zyada poochha jaane wala trap hai: /26 mein 64 total par 62 usable, /30 mein 4 total par sirf 2 usable.

**Galti 3: 127.0.0.1 ko Class A samajhna.** 127 se shuru hone wale address Class A range mein aate dikhte hain (1–126 ke baad 127), par 127 loopback ke liye reserved hai, Class A ka hissa nahi hai. Class A 1 se 126 tak hai, 127 alag hai. Exam mein yeh ek number ka farak poora answer badal deta hai.

**Galti 4: Private address ko public samajhna.** \`192.168.1.10\` internet par kaam nahi karta — yeh sirf aapke ghar ke andar kaam karta hai. Agar aap apne dost ko yeh address bhej kar bologe ki "meri website yahan hai", toh woh kabhi nahi kholega. Internet par sirf public address dikhta hai, jo router ko ISP deta hai. Private aur public ka farak samajhna NAT samajhne ki pehli seedhi hai.`,
          `The most common IP addressing mistakes happen in four places.

**Mistake 1: Giving the network or broadcast address to a device.** \`192.168.1.0\` and \`192.168.1.255\` cannot be given to any device — the first identifies the whole network, and the second sends a message to everyone. Beginners often treat .0 as the first host. The first usable host is always .1, never .0. If you give .0 to a laptop, the network will not work.

**Mistake 2: Forgetting to subtract 2 when counting usable hosts.** A /24 has 256 addresses in total, but only 254 are usable, not 256. Always subtract 2 — the network and the broadcast. This is the most common trap in GATE: a /26 has 64 total but 62 usable, and a /30 has 4 total but only 2 usable.

**Mistake 3: Treating 127.0.0.1 as Class A.** Addresses starting with 127 look as if they fall in the Class A range (after 1–126 comes 127), but 127 is reserved for loopback and is not part of Class A. Class A runs from 1 to 126; 127 is separate. In an exam, this one-number difference changes the whole answer.

**Mistake 4: Treating a private address as public.** \`192.168.1.10\` does not work on the internet — it works only inside your home. If you send this address to a friend and say "my website is here", it will never open. Only the public address is visible on the internet, and the ISP gives that to the router. Understanding the difference between private and public is the first step to understanding NAT.`
