          `Ab "what-if" sawaal poochhte hain.

**What if: classes hi use karte rahte?** Classful addressing mein network sirf 3 size mein milta tha: bada (Class A, 16 lakh hosts), beech wala (Class B, 65,000 hosts) ya chhota (Class C, 254 hosts). Agar aapko 500 hosts chahiye, toh Class B lena padta, jismein 65,000 mein se 64,500 addresses bekaar jaate. Isi barbadi ki wajah se classes ko chhoda gaya aur CIDR aaya, jo network ko kisi bhi size mein kaat sakta hai — /23 se 510 hosts, /25 se 126 hosts. CIDR ne IPv4 ki zindagi saalon badha di.

**What if: private addresses na hote?** Har ghar ke har device ko public address chahiye hota, aur IPv4 ke 4 arab addresses kab ke khatam ho jaate. Private addresses aur NAT ki wajah se ek ghar ke 20 devices ek hi public address se kaam chala lete hain. Private range hi woh wajah hai ki IPv4 aaj bhi chal raha hai, IPv6 ke aane ke baad bhi.

**What if: APIPA na hota?** DHCP fail hone par device ka koi address hi nahi hota aur do paas wale laptop aapas mein bhi baat nahi kar paate. APIPA ek backup plan hai: address na mile toh khud ek banao, taaki local kaam ruk na jaaye. Yeh perfect solution nahi hai, par kuch na hone se behtar hai.

**What if: multicast na hota?** Live class ya live match har student ko alag packet bhejna padta. 10,000 students par server ko 10,000 copies bhejni padti. Multicast mein ek copy network mein jaati hai aur network use sab tak pahuncha deta hai. Bina multicast ke live streaming itni sasti kabhi nahi hoti.`,
          `Now let us ask "what-if" questions.

**What if we had kept using classes?** With classful addressing, networks came in only 3 sizes: large (Class A, 16 million hosts), medium (Class B, 65,000 hosts), or small (Class C, 254 hosts). If you needed 500 hosts, you had to take Class B, wasting 64,500 of its 65,000 addresses. Because of this waste, classes were dropped and CIDR arrived, which can cut a network to any size — /23 for 510 hosts, /25 for 126 hosts. CIDR extended the life of IPv4 by years.

**What if there were no private addresses?** Every device in every home would need a public address, and the 4 billion IPv4 addresses would have run out long ago. Thanks to private addresses and NAT, 20 devices in one home manage with a single public address. The private ranges are the reason IPv4 still works today, even after IPv6 arrived.

**What if there were no APIPA?** If DHCP failed, a device would have no address at all, and two nearby laptops could not even talk to each other. APIPA is a backup plan: if no address arrives, make one yourself, so that local work does not stop. It is not a perfect solution, but it is better than nothing.

**What if there were no multicast?** A live class or a live match would need a separate packet for every student. For 10,000 students, the server would have to send 10,000 copies. With multicast, one copy enters the network and the network delivers it to everyone. Without multicast, live streaming would never have been this cheap.`
