          `**CDN (Content Delivery Network)** duniya bhar mein faile hue servers ka network hai, jo website ka content aapke paas wale server se deta hai. Bina CDN ke, India mein baitha user America ke **origin server** se har photo aur video maangta — har request ko samundar paar jaana padta aur page khulne mein der lagti. CDN ke saath, content ki copy pehle se hi aapke sheher ke paas wale **edge server** par rakhi hoti hai, isliye page turant khul jaata hai.

Teen shabd yaad rakho. **Origin server** woh asli server hai jahan website ka content pehli baar rakha hota hai. **Edge server** woh server hai jo aapke paas hai — CDN ke hazaron edge servers duniya bhar mein faile hote hain. **Cache** ka matlab hai copy ko save karke rakhna: jab pehla user kisi photo ko maangta hai, toh edge server use origin se lekar save kar leta hai; jab doosra user wahi photo maange, toh edge server apni copy se de deta hai, origin ko pareshan kiye bina.

Jab copy mil jaaye, toh use **cache HIT** kehte hain — fast aur sasta. Jab copy na mile, toh use **cache MISS** kehte hain — edge server ko origin se lena padta hai, isliye pehli baar thoda slow hota hai. **Hit rate** batata hai ki kitni requests HIT hui: 100 mein se 90 HIT ho toh hit rate 90% hai, aur yeh jitna zyada ho, CDN utna hi behtar kaam kar raha hai.`,
          `A **CDN (Content Delivery Network)** is a network of servers spread around the world that serves a website's content from the server nearest to you. Without a CDN, a user in India would fetch every photo and video from the **origin server** in America — every request would have to cross the ocean, and pages would open slowly. With a CDN, a copy of the content already sits on an **edge server** near your city, so the page opens at once.

Remember three words. The **origin server** is the real server where the website's content is first stored. The **edge server** is the server near you — a CDN has thousands of edge servers spread across the world. A **cache** means saving a copy: when the first user asks for a photo, the edge server fetches it from the origin and saves it; when a second user asks for the same photo, the edge server serves its own copy without bothering the origin.

When the copy is found, it is called a **cache HIT** — fast and cheap. When the copy is not found, it is called a **cache MISS** — the edge server must fetch it from the origin, so the first time is a little slow. The **hit rate** tells you how many requests were HITs: if 90 out of 100 are HITs, the hit rate is 90%, and the higher it is, the better the CDN is working.`
