          `Sabse bada example hai **YouTube**. Jab ek video viral hota hai, toh lakhon log use ek saath dekhte hain. Agar sab America ke ek server se video maangte, toh woh server gir jaata. CDN ki wajah se video ki copy India, Europe aur America ke edge servers par pehle se rakhi hoti hai — aapko video paas wale server se milta hai, isliye woh bina ruke chalta hai.

Doosra example hai **news website**. Jab koi badi khabar aati hai, toh ek hi minute mein lakhon log website kholte hain. Bina CDN ke origin server itni requests se gir jaata. CDN ke saath, khabar ka page edge servers par cache ho jaata hai aur lakhon logon ko edge se mil jaata hai — origin ko sirf ek baar content dena padta hai.

Teesra example hai **online shopping ki photo**. Ek product ki photo ko lakhon log dekhte hain, par photo kabhi badalti nahi. Isi liye photo ko edge par lambe time tak cache karke rakha jaata hai — har baar origin se photo maangna bekaar hota. Isi liye shopping websites itni fast khulti hain, chahe aap duniya ke kisi bhi kone mein hon.

Ek aur example hai **software update**. Jab phone ka naya update aata hai, toh crore log ek saath download karte hain. CDN update ki copy ko hazaron edge servers par baant deta hai, taaki koi ek server na gire aur har user ko fast download mile.`,
          `The biggest example is **YouTube**. When a video goes viral, millions of people watch it at the same time. If everyone fetched the video from one server in America, that server would fall over. Thanks to the CDN, copies of the video already sit on edge servers in India, Europe, and America — you get the video from a nearby server, so it plays without stopping.

A second example is a **news website**. When a big story breaks, millions of people open the website within a minute. Without a CDN, the origin server would fall over under so many requests. With a CDN, the story page is cached on edge servers and millions of people get it from the edge — the origin only has to serve the content once.

A third example is a **photo on an online shopping site**. Millions of people view one product photo, but the photo never changes. That is why the photo is cached on the edge for a long time — fetching it from the origin every time would be wasteful. That is why shopping websites open so fast, no matter where in the world you are.

One more example is a **software update**. When a new phone update arrives, tens of millions of people download it at once. The CDN spreads copies of the update across thousands of edge servers, so no single server falls over and every user gets a fast download.`
