          `Edge cases woh situations hain jo normal din mein nahi aati, par system ko yahi todti hain.

**1. Cache poora bhar gaya.** Edge server ki jagah khatam ho jaaye toh nayi file ke liye purani file nikaalni padti hai. LRU mein woh file nikalti hai jo sabse lambe time se use nahi hui. Agar aap ek hi baar dekhi gayi file ko cache karte rahoge, toh baar-baar dekhi jaane wali file nikal jaayegi — isliye kuch CDN sirf doosri baar maangi gayi file ko cache karte hain.

**2. Origin server gir gaya.** Agar origin down ho jaaye, toh edge server apni purani copy deta rehta hai, chahe TTL khatam ho gaya ho. Isi ko **stale-while-revalidate** ya **serve-stale** kehte hain — purani khabar, koi khabar na hone se behtar hai. Bina CDN ke origin girte hi website poori tarah band ho jaati.

**3. TTL khatam ho gaya.** TTL khatam hote hi agla request MISS ho jaata hai aur edge server origin se nayi copy leta hai. Is beech mein agar hazaron requests ek saath aayein, toh sab origin par toot padengi — isse **cache stampede** kehte hain. Isse bachne ke liye sirf ek request ko origin jaane diya jaata hai aur baaki ko purani copy di jaati hai.

**4. Pehla user (cold cache).** Jab edge server naya ho ya cache khaali ho, toh pehle user ko har cheez origin se leni padti hai — use **cold start** kehte hain. Pehla user slow experience karta hai, par uske baad sab fast ho jaata hai. Isi liye badi website pehle se hi popular content ko edge par bhar deti hai — isse **cache warming** kehte hain.`,
          `Edge cases are the situations that never appear on a normal day, but these are the ones that break a system.

**1. Cache is completely full.** When the edge server runs out of space, an old file must be removed to make room for a new one. With LRU, the file removed is the one that has not been used for the longest time. If you keep caching files that are viewed only once, the files viewed again and again get removed — that is why some CDNs only cache a file after it is asked for a second time.

**2. Origin server is down.** If the origin goes down, the edge server keeps serving its old copy, even if the TTL has expired. This is called **stale-while-revalidate** or **serve-stale** — old news is better than no news. Without a CDN, the website stops completely the moment the origin goes down.

**3. TTL has expired.** The moment the TTL ends, the next request is a MISS and the edge server fetches a fresh copy from the origin. If thousands of requests arrive together in that gap, they all rush to the origin — this is called a **cache stampede**. To prevent it, only one request is allowed to go to the origin while the rest get the old copy.

**4. The first user (cold cache).** When an edge server is new or its cache is empty, the first user must fetch everything from the origin — this is called a **cold start**. The first user gets a slow experience, but after that everything is fast. That is why large websites fill the edge with popular content in advance — this is called **cache warming**.`
