          `Sabse bada example hai **shopping sale**. Diwali sale mein ek hi minute mein lakhon log website kholte hain. Agar sirf ek server hota, toh woh gir jaata. Load balancer ke peeche 10 ya 20 server chalte hain, aur har request kisi ek server ko milti hai. Sale khatam hote hi server kam kar diye jaate hain — kiraya sirf utna hi, jitna use hua. Yahi cloud ki taakat hai: load badhe toh server badhao, load ghate toh server ghatao.

Doosra example hai **video streaming**. Jab aap video dekhte ho, toh request load balancer se hokar us server tak jaati hai jo sabse khaali hai. Agar ek server par bahut load hai, toh load balancer naye users ko doosre server par bhejta hai. Isi liye lakhon log ek saath video dekh paate hain bina ruke.

Teesra example hai **bank app**. Bank ka database kabhi internet se seedha nahi judta — woh VPC ke private subnet mein rehta hai, jahan sirf bank ke app server hi pahunch sakte hain. Bahar se koi bhi database ko chhoo nahi sakta. VPC hi woh deewar hai jo bank ke data ko internet se alag rakhti hai.

Ek aur example hai **startup ka pehla din**. Ek chhoti team bina ek bhi server khareede, VPC bana kar, usmein do server aur ek load balancer laga kar apni website chala leti hai. Server kharab ho toh naya server 5 minute mein ban jaata hai — pehle zamane mein naya server khareedne mein hafte lag jaate the.`,
          `The biggest example is a **shopping sale**. During a Diwali sale, millions of people open the website within a minute. If there were only one server, it would fall over. Behind the load balancer run 10 or 20 servers, and each request goes to one of them. Once the sale ends, the servers are reduced — you pay rent only for what you used. That is the strength of the cloud: when load grows, add servers; when load falls, remove servers.

A second example is **video streaming**. When you watch a video, the request goes through the load balancer to the server that is least busy. If one server is heavily loaded, the load balancer sends new users to another server. That is why millions of people can watch video at the same time without stopping.

A third example is a **bank app**. A bank's database is never joined straight to the internet — it sits in a private subnet of the VPC, where only the bank's app servers can reach it. Nobody from outside can touch the database. The VPC is the wall that keeps the bank's data separate from the internet.

One more example is a **startup's first day**. A small team, without buying a single server, creates a VPC, puts two servers and a load balancer in it, and runs its website. If a server breaks, a new one is ready in 5 minutes — in earlier days, buying a new server took weeks.`
