          `Ab load balancer ka kaam haath se dekhte hain. Hamare paas 3 server hain: web-1, web-2 aur web-3. **Round-robin** ka matlab hai baari-baari dena — pehli request web-1 ko, doosri web-2 ko, teesri web-3 ko, chauthi phir web-1 ko, aur aise hi aage.

9 requests aati hain:
- **Request 1, 4, 7 → web-1.** Har teesri request web-1 ko milti hai.
- **Request 2, 5, 8 → web-2.**
- **Request 3, 6, 9 → web-3.**

Aakhir mein har server ko 3-3 requests mili — load poori tarah barabar baant gaya. Yeh hisaab simple hai: server number = (request number - 1) % 3. Request 7 ke liye (7 - 1) % 3 = 0, matlab pehla server web-1. Yeh wahi modulo hisaab hai jo hashing mein bhi aata hai — ek hi idea, do jagah.

Par round-robin tabhi achha hai jab saare server barabar taakat ke hon aur har request barabar bhaari ho. Agar web-1 kamzor server hai, toh use bhi 3 requests milengi aur woh slow ho jaayega. Isi liye aadhunik load balancer **least-connections** bhi use karte hain — request us server ko do jiske paas abhi sabse kam kaam hai. Neeche wala code yahi dry run print karta hai.`,
          `Now let us see the load balancer's work by hand. We have 3 servers: web-1, web-2, and web-3. **Round-robin** means giving in turn — the first request to web-1, the second to web-2, the third to web-3, the fourth back to web-1, and so on.

Nine requests arrive:
- **Requests 1, 4, 7 → web-1.** Every third request goes to web-1.
- **Requests 2, 5, 8 → web-2.**
- **Requests 3, 6, 9 → web-3.**

In the end, every server got 3 requests each — the load was split completely evenly. The calculation is simple: server number = (request number - 1) % 3. For request 7, (7 - 1) % 3 = 0, meaning the first server, web-1. This is the same modulo calculation that appears in hashing — one idea, two places.

But round-robin is good only when all servers are equally strong and every request is equally heavy. If web-1 is a weak server, it still gets 3 requests and becomes slow. That is why modern load balancers also use **least-connections** — give the request to the server that currently has the least work. The code below prints this dry run.`
