          `Let us tie the whole article together. **Cloud networking** means joining rented servers with your own network. A **VPC** is your own private network inside the cloud — you can make subnets in it, and you can lock the database in a private subnet so that the internet cannot touch it.

A **load balancer** spreads incoming requests across many servers. **Round-robin** shares in turn — 9 requests, 3 servers, 3 each. A **health check** tells you which server is alive; a dead server is removed from the list and gets no requests. **Least-connections** gives the request to the server that currently has the least work.

Remember: a load balancer helps only when there are at least 2 servers behind it. Always keep the database in a private subnet. And keep a backup at every layer — never trust one server, one region, or one load balancer.

To revise, answer these three questions without looking: with 10 requests and 2 servers, how many requests does each server get in round-robin? What is the job of a health check? Why should a database never be kept in a public subnet? If you can answer all three without stopping, you know cloud networking. In the next article we will do a project — building a subnet calculator in Python.`
