          `Ab "what-if" sawaal poochhte hain.

**What if: cache na hota?** Har baar har naam ke liye poora safar karna padta — root, TLD, authoritative. Har website khulne mein der lagti aur DNS server par load bahut badh jaata. Cache hi woh wajah hai ki dobara kholi gayi website fast khulti hai aur DNS server lakhon requests sambhal leta hai.

**What if: hosts file na hoti?** Localhost jaise naam kaam nahi karte. Developer ko apne computer par website test karne ke liye har baar 127.0.0.1 likhna padta, aur office ke server ke liye IP yaad rakhna padta. Hosts file chhoti hai, par local naam ke liye sabse fast tareeka hai — usmein koi network hi nahi lagta.

**What if: TTL hamesha ke liye hota?** Toh IP badalne par bhi purana IP milta rehta. Website ne server badla, par user purane server par jaata rehta aur website band dikhti. TTL hi woh balance hai jo fast aur fresh ke beech mein rakha jaata hai — CDN mein bhi wahi balance tha.

**What if: resolver seedha authoritative server se poochhe?** Toh use pehle yeh pata hona chahiye ki authoritative server kahan hai — aur yeh jaankari sirf TLD server ke paas hai, aur TLD ki jaankari sirf root ke paas hai. Isi liye safar root se shuru hota hai. Bina root ke, resolver ko har domain ka server pehle se yaad rakhna padta, jo namumkin hai.`,
          `Now let us ask "what-if" questions.

**What if there were no cache?** Every name would need the full journey every time — root, TLD, authoritative. Every website would open slowly, and the load on DNS servers would grow very large. The cache is the reason a website opened again loads fast and a DNS server handles millions of requests.

**What if there were no hosts file?** Names such as localhost would not work. A developer testing a website on their own computer would have to type 127.0.0.1 every time, and would have to remember the IP of the office server. The hosts file is small, but it is the fastest way for local names — it needs no network at all.

**What if the TTL lasted forever?** Then the old IP would keep coming back even after the IP changed. The website changed its server, but the user would keep going to the old server and the website would look down. The TTL is the balance kept between fast and fresh — the same balance as in a CDN.

**What if the resolver asked the authoritative server directly?** Then it would first need to know where the authoritative server is — and only the TLD server knows that, and only the root knows the TLD. That is why the journey starts at the root. Without the root, the resolver would have to remember the server for every domain in advance, which is impossible.`
