          `**Interview prep** does not mean memorising questions; it means understanding the reason behind them. An interviewer does not check your definitions, they check your understanding. They will ask "what is the difference between TCP and UDP?" — and if you only say "TCP is reliable, UDP is fast", they will ask next "what does reliable mean? How does it become reliable?" That next question is the interview.

CN questions split into 6 big topics. **First: the OSI and TCP/IP models** — 7 layers, the job of each layer, and what happens at which layer. **Second: TCP vs UDP** — reliability, connection, speed, and where each one is used. **Third: IP addressing and subnetting** — classes, usable hosts, /24, /30, and CIDR. **Fourth: DNS** — the journey from a name to an IP, cache, and TTL. **Fifth: Routing** — how a router sends a packet, and what a routing table is. **Sixth: the application layer** — HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, and their port numbers.

In this article we will take one top question from each topic, give the correct answer, and also give the reason behind the answer. At the end we will do GATE-style numerical problems — bandwidth-delay product and transmission time — and make a 7-day revision plan.`
